ests call it as `proverb(*inputs, qualifier=...)`.\n\n- Each consecutive pair of items produces one \"For want of a X the Y was lost.\" line.\n- The final line is \"And all for the want of a {qualifier} {first}.\" when a qualifier is given, and \"And all for the want of a {first}.\" otherwise.\n- An empty input returns an empty list.\n- The function returns a list of lines, which is what the Exercism Python tests expect.\n\nproverb.py\n```python\ndef proverb(*inputs, qualifier=None):\n    if not inputs:\n        return []\n    lines = [\n        f\"For want of a {first} the {second} was lost.\"\n        for first, second in zip(inputs, inputs[1:])\n    ]\n    start = f\"{qualifier} {inputs[0]}\" if qualifier else inputs[0]\n    lines.append(f\"And all for the want of a {start}.\")\n    return lines\n```", "blocks": [{"index": 0, "type": "text", "chars": 921, "signature": false}], "events": 66, "event_types": {"message_start": 1, "content_block_start": 1, "ping": 1, "content_block_delta": 60, "content_block_stop": 1, "message_delta": 1, "message_stop": 1}}
